{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Ecosystems have unmodeled diversity generation mechanisms; software services may intentionally copy specs, collapsing diversity.","Biological common causes can co-evolve with their targets; software specs are usually fixed and externally chosen."],"overturn_conditions":["Show that spec-heterogeneous large services can achieve the same decorrelation as decentralized heterogeneous networks.","Show that observed decorrelation persists even under shared spec, implying spec is not the dominant causal driver.","Show that the ecosystem analogy fails because the relevant causal structures in software are spec/dependency rather than species/resource."],"shared_structure":["Species diversity in an ecosystem lowers correlated-collapse risk only when species differ in resource needs and vulnerabilities, not merely in location.","Monocultures, even spatially separated, collapse together when a matching pathogen arrives; spatial redundancy without spec diversity fails.","The hidden causal coupling in both cases is the shared specification/species-type, not the visible separation of replicas."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_004"],"transfer_claims":["Decentralized services should be evaluated by the diversity of their specification and dependency graphs, not by their count or operator count.","Evidence of independence must include specification and dependency divergence, not merely observed decorrelation.","A large service with spec-heterogeneous redundant paths may be more resilient than a decentralized network of spec-homogeneous small services."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A large service's latent-yet-untriggered redundancy does not actually lower the probability of correlated failure if the common cause is a specification or upstream dependency shared by all redundant paths; independence must be defined causally, not statistically, and this favors decentralized networks only when they have heterogeneous specifications and non-overlapping dependency cones.","counterconditions":["If the large service's redundant components are deliberately heterogeneous in spec and dependencies, the argument against it weakens.","If small services independently converge on the same specification and the same upstream dependency, their correlation may be as high as replicas.","If correlation only appears statistically uncorrelated because the shared cause is rare, the argument fails when the rare event is triggered.","If correlation is not specification-driven but purely environmental, decentralization may not help.","If small services are operated by the same entity or share configuration conventions, their independence may be nominal."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Decoherence via shared spec: if redundant modules or replicas share a specification, their independent implementations still converge to the same vulnerability class (spec-driven common-cause failure). In a centralized service, extra replicas add homogeneous copies sharing the same specification, dependencies, and maintainer; the event space contains hidden causal couplings that only vanish when the alternatives are generated under different specs and different dependency cones. Thus the relevant unit of redundancy is not replica count but the diversity of the specification graph. Decentralization, when accompanied by heterogeneous specs, actually reduces correlated failure; when accompanied by identical specs (or strong social mimicry), it does not.","optional_refs":["8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72}],"simulation_proposals":[]}